{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '82,102p' 'content/legal-system-research-and-reference/Case Index.md'; sed -n '190,212p' 'content/legal-system-research-and-reference/Case Index.md'; sed -n '488,510p' 'content/legal-system-research-and-reference/Case Index.md'\"","aggregated_output":"| [[California v. Acevedo]] | Unified rule for containers in vehicles: police may search a container in a car without a warrant where they have PC to believe it holds… | good | [[Searching Effects and Containers]] · [[Automobile Exception]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112608/california-v-acevedo/) |\n| [[California v. Beheler]] | A suspect who is not under arrest, voluntarily comes to the police station, and is allowed to leave after a brief interview is not 'in custody' for Miranda purposes; the ultimate custody inquiry is whether there was a formal arrest or restraint on freedom of movement of the degree associated with a formal arrest. | good | [[Miranda and Custodial Interrogation]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/111023/california-v-beheler/) |\n| [[California v. Carney]] | The automobile exception applies to a motor home being used as a vehicle, and articulates the exception's TWO justifications: (1) ready… | good | [[Automobile Exception]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/111423/california-v-carney/) |\n| [[California v. Ciraolo]] | Warrantless naked-eye aerial observation of a fenced [[Curtilage\\|curtilage]] from navigable airspace (1,000 ft) is not a search — no [[Reasonable Expectation of Privacy\\|reasonable expectation of privacy]] from the air. | good | [[Aerial and Enhanced Surveillance]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/111666/california-v-ciraolo/) |\n| [[California v. Greenwood]] | No [[Reasonable Expectation of Privacy\\|reasonable expectation of privacy]] in garbage bags left for collection at the curb, outside the [[Curtilage\\|curtilage]]; warrantless search/seizure of curbside trash does not violate the 4A. | good | [[Abandonment]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112067/california-v-greenwood/) |\n| [[California v. Hodari D.]] | A show-of-authority seizure is not complete until the suspect submits; contraband discarded while still fleeing is not the fruit of a seizure. | good | [[Seizure of the Person]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112579/california-v-hodari-d/) |\n| [[California v. Prysock]] | [[Miranda and Custodial Interrogation\\|Miranda warnings]] need not be a verbatim recital of the language in Miranda; a warning that reasonably conveys the suspect's rights is adequate — no talismanic incantation is required. | good | [[Miranda and Custodial Interrogation]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/110556/california-v-prysock/) |\n| [[California v. Trombetta]] | The Due Process Clause does not require law enforcement to preserve breath samples taken from suspected drunk drivers, because the constitutional duty to preserve evidence reaches only evidence whose [[Brady and Giglio\\|exculpatory]] value was apparent before it was destroyed and that the defendant cannot replace by other reasonably available means. | good | [[Brady and Giglio]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/111206/california-v-trombetta/) |\n| [[Camara v. Municipal Court]] | Administrative inspections of private property generally require a warrant, but it may be an \"area warrant\" issued on reasonable… | good | [[Special Needs and Administrative Searches]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/107473/camara-v-municipal-court-of-city-and-county-of-san-francisco/) |\n| [[Caniglia v. Strom]] | There is NO freestanding '[[Community Caretaking\\|community caretaking]]' exception authorizing warrantless entry into the HOME. Cady's caretaking rationale was… | good | [[Emergency Aid]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4883694/caniglia-v-strom/) |\n| [[Cardwell v. Lewis]] | Examining a car's exterior (paint scrapings, tire tread) on probable cause in a public lot invades no privacy interest the warrant requirement protects; one has a reduced expectation of privacy in a vehicle, especially its exterior. | good | [[Automobile Exception]] · [[Reasonable Expectation of Privacy]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/109069/cardwell-v-lewis/) |\n| [[Carpenter v. United States]] | Acquiring extended historical cell-site location information is a search — a [[Reasonable Expectation of Privacy\\|reasonable expectation of privacy]] in 'the whole of [one's] physical movements'; narrows the third-party doctrine for digital-age data. | good | [[Reasonable Expectation of Privacy]] · [[Third-Party Doctrine & CSLI]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4510032/carpenter-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Carroll v. Carman]] | It is not clearly established that the 'knock and talk' exception requires officers to approach only the front door; an officer who went to a side sliding-glass door that visitors could use was therefore entitled to [[Qualified Immunity\\|qualified immunity]], and the Supreme Court left open whether such an approach is constitutional. | good | [[Knock and Talk]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2750102/carroll-v-carman/) |\n| [[Carroll v. United States]] | Origin of the automobile exception: a vehicle may be searched without a warrant on probable cause because, unlike a fixed structure, it… | good | [[Automobile Exception]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/100567/carroll-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Carter v. United States]] | A man was seized under the Fourth Amendment when an officer, backed by a show of authority and after disbelieving his response, directed him to hike up his pants; on the objective free-to-terminate inquiry — which properly accounts for the reasonable apprehension of a Black man in a heavily policed encounter — that request occurred before reasonable suspicion arose, making the seizure and its fruits unlawful. | good | [[Seizure of the Person]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10662535/carter-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Case Index]] |  | good |  | — |\n| [[Case v. Montana]] | Brigham City's objective-reasonableness standard for warrantless home entries to render [[Emergency Aid\\|emergency aid]] applies without further gloss — it is neither lowered to Terry reasonable suspicion nor raised to probable cause — and asks only whether an officer had an objectively reasonable basis for believing entry was needed to prevent or deal with serious harm. | good | [[Emergency Aid]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10774335/case-v-montana/) |\n| [[Chambers v. Florida]] | Confessions extracted through prolonged, incommunicado interrogation of helpless prisoners were the product of compulsion and their use… | good | [[Due-Process Voluntariness of Confessions]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/103301/chambers-v-florida/) |\n| [[Chambers v. Maroney]] | Where there was PC and mobility at the scene, officers may search the vehicle without a warrant later at the station house; immediate… | good | [[Automobile Exception]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/108184/chambers-v-maroney/) |\n| [[Chandler v. Miller]] | Georgia's suspicionless drug-testing requirement for candidates for state office is unconstitutional — there was no concrete, special… | good | [[Special Needs and Administrative Searches]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/118100/chandler-v-miller/) |\n| [[Chapman v. United States (1961)]] | A landlord cannot give valid [[Consent Searches\\|third-party consent]] to a search of premises currently leased to a tenant; a warrantless entry of the tenant's home on the landlord's authority alone violates the Fourth Amendment. | good | [[Consent Searches]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/106197/chapman-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Gouled v. United States]] | Mere-evidence rule: warrants may seize only contraband, fruits, or instrumentalities, not items of solely evidentiary value (overruled by Warden v. Hayden); entry obtained by stealth or ruse can render a search unreasonable. | good | [[Trespass]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/99745/gouled-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Grady v. North Carolina]] | A State conducts a Fourth Amendment search when it attaches a satellite-based monitoring device to a person's body, without consent, to track his movements; the civil character of the monitoring program does not remove the conduct from the Fourth Amendment, leaving only the reasonableness of the search for decision [[Reading and Citing Cases#on-remand\\|on remand]]. | good | [[Trespass]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2789928/grady-v-north-carolina/) |\n| [[Graham v. Barnette]] | After Caniglia v. Strom, using the '[[Community Caretaking\\|community caretaking]]' label for warrantless psychiatric seizures is a category error; a seizure of a person for an emergency mental-health evaluation is reasonable under the Fourth Amendment only on probable cause that the person is mentally ill and dangerous to herself or others (though the officers received [[Qualified Immunity\\|qualified immunity]] because that standard was not clearly established in the circuit). | good | [[Community Caretaking]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4900401/teresa-graham-v-shannon-barnette/) |\n| [[Graham v. Connor]] | Excessive-force § 1983 claims arising from an arrest, stop, or other seizure are analyzed under the Fourth Amendment's 'objective… | good | [[Use of Force]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112257/graham-v-connor/) |\n| [[Griffin v. Wisconsin]] | A warrantless search of a probationer's home pursuant to a valid regulation is reasonable when supported by \"reasonable grounds\";… | good | [[Special Needs and Administrative Searches]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/111959/griffin-v-wisconsin/) |\n| [[Groh v. Ramirez]] | A warrant that utterly **fails to describe the persons or things to be seized** is facially invalid under the [[Particularity]] Clause —… | good | [[Particularity]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/131161/groh-v-ramirez/) |\n| [[Gutierrez v. Saenz]] | A death-sentenced prisoner has Article III standing to bring a § 1983 procedural-due-process challenge to a State's postconviction DNA-testing scheme where a favorable judgment would redress his injury by removing the state prosecutor's asserted reason for denying DNA testing, even though the testing itself would not necessarily lead to his release. | good | [[Section 1983 Liability and Qualified Immunity]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10776824/gutierrez-v-saenz/) |\n| [[Hafer v. Melo]] | State officials sued in their individual (personal) capacities for actions taken in their official capacity are 'persons' within the meaning of § 1983; the Eleventh Amendment does not bar such personal-capacity suits, and the official character of the challenged acts does not by itself confer absolute immunity. | good | [[Section 1983 Liability and Qualified Immunity]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112657/hafer-v-melo/) |\n| [[Hampton v. United States]] | Neither the entrapment defense nor the Due Process Clause bars conviction of a PREDISPOSED defendant even where a government agent… | good | [[Entrapment]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/109437/hampton-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Hanlon v. Berger]] | A media ride-along during the execution of a search warrant violated the Fourth Amendment under Wilson v. Layne, but the officers were entitled to [[Qualified Immunity\\|qualified immunity]]. | good | [[Qualified Immunity]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1087699/hanlon-v-berger/) |\n| [[Harlow v. Fitzgerald]] | Reformulated [[Qualified Immunity\\|qualified immunity]] as a purely OBJECTIVE test: officials performing discretionary functions are shielded from civil damages… | good | [[Qualified Immunity]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/110763/harlow-v-fitzgerald/) |\n| [[Harris v. New York]] | A statement taken in violation of Miranda, but otherwise voluntary, may be used to impeach the defendant's contrary trial testimony; Miranda's shield may not be turned into a license to commit perjury free from confrontation with prior inconsistent statements. | good | [[Miranda Waiver and Invocation]] · [[Fruits & Attenuation]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/108272/harris-v-new-york/) |\n| [[Harris v. United States (1968)]] | Objects falling in the plain view of an officer who has a right to be in the position to have that view are subject to seizure and admissible; a protective measure taken to secure a lawfully impounded car is not a search. | good | [[Plain View Doctrine]] · [[Automobile Exception]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/107625/harris-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Hayes v. Florida]] | Transporting a suspect to the station for fingerprinting without consent, a warrant, or probable cause is a seizure tantamount to arrest requiring probable cause (brief field fingerprinting on reasonable suspicion left open). | good | [[Seizure of the Person]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/111382/hayes-v-florida/) |\n| [[Haynes v. Washington]] | A written confession obtained in an atmosphere of substantial coercion and inducement — incommunicado detention plus the express threat of continued isolation and the promise of contact with family conditioned on signing a confession — is involuntary under the totality of circumstances and inadmissible under the Fourteenth Amendment. | good | [[Due-Process Voluntariness of Confessions]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/106625/haynes-v-washington/) |\n| [[Heck v. Humphrey]] | A § 1983 damages claim whose success would necessarily imply the invalidity of an outstanding conviction or sentence is not cognizable unless the conviction has first been reversed, expunged, declared invalid, or called into question by [[Common Legal Terms#habeas-corpus\\|habeas]] (the favorable-termination requirement). | good | [[Section 1983 Liability and Qualified Immunity]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/117864/heck-v-humphrey/) |\n| [[Heien v. North Carolina]] | A traffic stop is valid if based on an officer's objectively reasonable mistake of law (as well as a reasonable mistake of fact); a… | good | [[Traffic Stops]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2760668/heien-v-north-carolina/) |\n| [[Heller v. New York]] | A single copy of an allegedly obscene film may be seized as evidence under a warrant issued by a neutral magistrate on a probable-cause determination without a prior adversary hearing on obscenity, provided a prompt post-seizure adversary hearing is available and, if the seizure would halt exhibition, the exhibitor is allowed to copy the film. | good | [[Particularity]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/108853/heller-v-new-york/) |\n| [[Henry v. United States (1959)]] | Probable cause for a warrantless arrest is judged by the facts known at the moment of arrest; outwardly innocent conduct (mere package movement) does not supply it, and an arrest is not justified by what the subsequent search discloses. | good | [[Probable Cause]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/105963/henry-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Hernandez v. Mesa]] | Bivens does not extend to a damages claim arising from a cross-border shooting: such a claim arises in a markedly new context with foreign-relations and national-security implications, and Congress's reluctance to create remedies for tortious conduct abroad counsels against implying a cause of action. | good | [[Suing Federal Officers]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9231296/hernandez-v-mesa/) |\n| [[Herring v. United States]] | Suppression is warranted only where deterrence benefits outweigh costs; isolated, attenuated police negligence (a recordkeeping error)… | good | [[The Good-Faith Exception]] · [[Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/145922/herring-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Hester v. United States]] | Origin of the open-fields doctrine — 4A protection of 'persons, houses, papers, and effects' does not extend to open fields; and a fleeing suspect who drops containers abandons any 4A interest in them. | good | [[Open Fields]] · [[Curtilage]] · [[Abandonment]] · [[Abandonment]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/100413/hester-v-united-states/) |\n| [[Hiibel v. Sixth Judicial Dist. Court]] | A state stop-and-identify law compelling a suspect to give his name during a valid *Terry* stop is consistent with the Fourth Amendment. | good | [[Stop-and-Identify]] · [[Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/136990/hiibel-v-sixth-judicial-dist-court-of-nev-humboldt-cty/) |\n| [[United States v. Conner]] | Where police, under color of authority, demand that occupants of a motel room open the door, and an occupant opens the door not… | good | [[Securing the Scene]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/747208/united-states-v-larry-duane-conner-united-states-of-america-v-john/) |\n| [[United States v. Cooley]] | A tribal police officer has authority to detain temporarily and to search a non-Indian traveling on a public right-of-way running through an Indian reservation for potential violations of state or federal law. | good | [[Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4887958/united-states-v-cooley/) |\n| [[United States v. Cortez]] | Reasonable suspicion = a particularized and objective basis on the [[Common Legal Terms#totality-of-the-circumstances\\|totality of the circumstances]] (the 'whole picture'). | good | [[Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion]] · [[Reasonable Suspicion]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/110377/united-states-v-cortez/) |\n| [[United States v. Cotterman]] | A forensic (comprehensive) examination of an electronic device seized at the border requires reasonable suspicion; it is the… | good | [[Border Searches]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/854692/united-states-v-howard-cotterman/) |\n| [[United States v. Crews]] | A victim's in-court identification of the accused is not a suppressible fruit of his illegal arrest where the victim's presence and her ability to identify him have an [[Inevitable Discovery and Independent Source\\|independent source]] predating the police misconduct. | good | [[Inevitable Discovery & Independent Source]] · [[Eyewitness Identification]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/110230/united-states-v-crews/) |\n| [[United States v. Crumble]] | A person who flees a wrecked car and leaves his cell phone behind, then denies any knowledge of the vehicle, abandons the phone and forfeits any [[Reasonable Expectation of Privacy\\|reasonable expectation of privacy]] in it — judged by the objective facts available to officers, not the owner's subjective intent — and the abandonment doctrine applies to cell phones notwithstanding Riley v. California, so the warrantless seizure and later search of the phone did not violate the Fourth Amendment. | good | [[Abandonment]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4456532/united-states-v-prentiss-anthony-crumble/) |\n| *United States v. Cruz / United States v. West / second bare 'United States v. Jackson' — UNVERIFIABLE as captioned; West REMOVED 2026-07-06* | Three bare names with no [[Reading and Citing Cases#reporter\\|reporter]], pinpoint, or holding detail appear in the capture under the good-faith/bad-faith-warrant heading. *West* (claimed for a stolen-vehicle/no-standing proposition) was re-verified by dual-model web search (packet A): no matching authority exists — removed with user sign-off; the true doctrine point is carried by [[Byrd v. United States]]. *Cruz* remains a page-less flagged exception (R10 carry-forward); the second bare *Jackson* is adjudicated in its Exclusionary-Rule context at the S7 pass. | ⚠ unverifiable | [[The Exclusionary Rule]] | — |\n| [[United States v. Daniels]] | The [[Common Legal Terms#totality-of-the-circumstances\\|totality of the circumstances]] did not establish reasonable suspicion to detain Daniels: a near-anonymous, non-emergency tip that alleged no illegality and described men in dark clothing — which Daniels, in a bright orange jumpsuit, did not match — plus his mere proximity to the described SUV in a high-crime area late at night amounted only to an arbitrary hunch, so suppressing his name as the fruit of the unlawful detention was proper. | good | [[Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion]] · [[Reasonable Suspicion]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9500360/united-states-v-daniels/) |\n| [[United States v. Donovan]] | Title III's identification requirement, § 2518(1)(b)(iv), obliges the Government to name in a wiretap application every person it has probable cause to believe is committing the offense and whose communications will be intercepted, and § 2518(8)(d) requires it to give the issuing judge a complete list of identifiable persons overheard so the judge can decide who receives inventory notice; but the failure to comply fully with either provision does not render the interception 'unlawful' and does not require suppression, because those requirements do not directly and substantially implement Congress's purpose of confining wiretaps to situations that clearly call for them. | good | [[Electronic Surveillance and Title III]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/109584/united-states-v-donovan/) |\n| [[United States v. Drayton]] | A bus sweep with consent-to-search requests is not a seizure, and consent can be voluntary even though officers do not advise passengers… | good | [[Knock and Talk]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/121153/united-states-v-drayton/) |\n| [[United States v. Dunn]] | [[Curtilage]] is determined by four factors — proximity to the home, whether the area is within an enclosure surrounding the home, the nature of its use, and steps taken to shield it from observation — all bearing on whether the area is so intimately tied to the home as to fall under the home's Fourth Amendment umbrella. | good | [[Curtilage]] · [[Open Fields]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/111833/united-states-v-dunn/) |\n| [[United States v. Edwards]] | A [[Search Incident to Arrest\\|search incident to arrest]] may extend in time: clothing and effects in an arrestee's possession that were subject to search at the time of arrest may be seized and examined without a warrant at the jail, even after a substantial, reasonable delay. | good | [[SIA Persons]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/108995/united-states-v-edwards/) |\n| [[United States v. Evans]] | UPHELD an inventory search of a carry-on bag (cocaine found in a container) conducted at a bus station: the officer followed the… | good | [[Inventory Searches]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/564407/united-states-v-daryl-lee-evans/) |\n| [[United States v. Flores-Montano]] | The government's authority to conduct suspicionless searches of vehicles at the border includes disassembling and reassembling a gas… | good | [[Border Searches]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/134729/united-states-v-flores-montano/) |\n| [[United States v. Ganias]] | Sitting [[Reading and Citing Cases#en-banc\\|en banc]], the Second Circuit affirmed Ganias's tax-evasion conviction on good-faith grounds — the agents' reliance on the 2006 warrant to search forensic mirror images retained from a 2003 search was objectively reasonable under Leon — and therefore expressly declined to decide whether the Government's years-long retention of non-responsive mirrored computer data beyond the 2003 warrant's scope violated the Fourth Amendment, displacing the panel's contrary holding. | good | [[Plain View Doctrine]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/3207604/united-states-v-ganias/) |\n| [[United States v. Garner]] | A community-caretaking detention of a person is valid under a three-part test — (1) specific and articulable facts warranting the intrusion, (2) the government's caretaking interest outweighing the individual's liberty interest, and (3) scope and duration tailored to the caretaking purpose; once that purpose is satisfied, continued detention requires independent reasonable suspicion. | good | [[Community Caretaking]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/166206/united-states-v-garner/) |\n| [[United States v. Gastiaburo]] | The automobile exception is not subject to a temporal limit; a 38-day gap between the car's seizure and the warrantless search did not… | good | [[Automobile Exception]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/7027957/united-states-v-gastiaburo/) |\n| [[United States v. Giordano]] | Under 18 U.S.C. § 2516(1), only the Attorney General or an Assistant Attorney General specially designated by him may authorize a Title III wiretap application; where an application was in fact approved by the Attorney General's Executive Assistant rather than a statutorily designated official, the interception was 'unlawfully intercepted' and the evidence — including evidence derived under a later extension order — must be suppressed, because the senior-approval requirement directly and substantially implements Congress's purpose of confining wiretaps to situations that clearly warrant them. | good | [[Electronic Surveillance and Title III]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/109020/united-states-v-giordano/) |\n| [[United States v. Gooch]] | (Persuasive (outside circuit) — 9th Cir.) An occupant has a [[Reasonable Expectation of Privacy\\|reasonable expectation of privacy]] in a tent in a public campground; 'a tent is more like a house than a car,' so its warrantless search violated the 4A. | good | [[Tents]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/654273/united-states-v-kenneth-d-gooch/) |\n| [[United States v. Gouveia]] | The Sixth Amendment right to counsel attaches only at or after the initiation of adversary judicial proceedings (formal charge, preliminary hearing, indictment, information, or arraignment); inmates held in administrative segregation during a preindictment investigation have no Sixth Amendment right to counsel. | good | [[Sixth Amendment Right to Counsel]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/111193/united-states-v-gouveia/) |\n| [[United States v. Grubbs]] | **Anticipatory warrants** — warrants that take effect only upon a future 'triggering condition' — are not categorically… | good | [[Probable Cause in the Affidavit]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/145670/united-states-v-grubbs/) |\n| [[United States v. Hanapel]] | The Eighth Circuit affirmed the denial of judgment of acquittal, holding that Hanapel failed to establish entrapment as a matter of law: there was no government inducement as a matter of law, and his initial hesitation on learning the decoy's age did not negate the predisposition a reasonable jury could find from his ready pursuit of the opportunity — arriving at the meeting place with condoms within hours. | good | [[Entrapment]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10038262/united-states-v-james-hanapel/) |\n| [[United States v. Harris (1971)]] | An informant's statement against his penal interest is itself an indicium of reliability that can support probable cause for a warrant; admissions of crime 'carry their own indicia of credibility,' and a magistrate may also rely on an officer's knowledge of the suspect's reputation. | good | [[Probable Cause]] · [[Probable Cause in the Affidavit]] | [opinion](https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/108379/united-states-v-harris/) |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
