{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0061\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later decision was found deciding whether Kopchak's cupped-hands contact with the vehicle window was an unlawful trespass; Loines remains a no-reach disposition on that issue.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Loines cupping hands window trespass and plain view window trespass alternative ground; reviewed United States v. Loines and later citing decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0062\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court or Fourth Circuit decision was found extending collective knowledge to uncommunicated horizontal aggregation. Massenburg remains controlling in the Fourth Circuit; no relevant certiorari grant was located.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched collective knowledge horizontal aggregation and Massenburg no case expanded collective knowledge; checked recent appellate decisions and Supreme Court dockets.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0063\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No subsequent May-Shaw proceeding or controlling decision resolved the independent-source or good-faith alternatives that the Sixth Circuit did not reach.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched May-Shaw independent source good faith not reached and pole camera curtilage alternative grounds; reviewed May-Shaw and later citations.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0064\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Seventh Circuit still has not decided whether a forensic DOMEX extraction requires reasonable suspicion. United States v. Eta addressed a manual search, not the reserved forensic-search standard.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched forensic DOMEX extraction reasonable suspicion and Mendez forensic border search; reviewed United States v. Mendez and United States v. Eta.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0065\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Mendez's forensic-search standard remains open. Recent Fourth, Seventh, and Eleventh Circuit decisions preserve rather than resolve the differing approaches to forensic border-device searches.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched forensic border device search standard split and Mendez Kolsuz Cano Touset; reviewed Mendez, Eta, Nkongho, and Pulido.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0066\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later First Circuit decision resolved Moore-Bush's evenly divided en banc disposition, so it still establishes no binding First Circuit rule on whether prolonged pole-camera surveillance is a search.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched pole camera surveillance search no binding circuit rule and Moore-Bush en banc 3-3; checked later First Circuit opinions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0067\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The First Circuit has issued no precedential post-Moore-Bush opinion adopting either side of the pole-camera search question.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Moore-Bush no binding circuit rule pole camera and First Circuit pole camera search; checked First Circuit opinions and citing authorities.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0068\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The pole-camera-as-search issue remains unresolved in the First Circuit despite decisions from other circuits addressing their own surveillance records.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched pole camera search unresolved First Circuit and Moore-Bush open question; reviewed later circuit decisions including Harry, Gregory, and Green.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0069\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later controlling Fifth Circuit decision was found deciding the Fourth Amendment merits that the en banc Morton court bypassed on good-faith grounds.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Morton declined Fourth Amendment phone warrant and en banc good faith phone search; reviewed Morton and later Fifth Circuit phone-search cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0070\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Neither the Supreme Court nor the Fifth Circuit has adopted a distinct digital exception to plain view that resolves Morton's reserved warrant-scope problem.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched plain view digital exception unresolved and Morton concurrence data warrant scope; reviewed later digital-search decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0071\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later controlling merits decision was found deciding whether Morton's warrants were overbroad as to the phones' photographs.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Morton overbroad phone photo warrant and particularity phone photographs; reviewed Morton and later Fifth Circuit citations.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0072\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later Perez disposition was found reaching the government's Maldonado-Espinosa alternative ground; the First Circuit panel's no-reach statement remains current.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Perez alternative Maldonado-Espinosa ground and backpack search incident alternative ground; reviewed Perez and subsequent history.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0073\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The hash-match private-search tension remains live and has deepened: Maher and Lowers follow Wilson's narrower approach, while Brillhart follows Reddick and Miller. No Supreme Court resolution or grant was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched hash match private search circuit split and Reddick Wilson Miller; reviewed United States v. Maher, United States v. Lowers, and United States v. Brillhart.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0074\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Robinson's en banc rule remains binding in the Fourth Circuit, while other courts continue to require facts showing dangerousness beyond firearm possession. No Supreme Court decision resolved the competing formulations.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched armed dangerous frisk Terry split and Robinson open carry frisk split; checked recent appellate decisions and certiorari dockets.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0075\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court still has not applied the due-process theory reserved in United States v. Russell to overturn a conviction for outrageous government conduct.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched outrageous government conduct due process limit and Russell left open due process defense; checked recent Supreme Court merits cases and grants.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0076\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The disagreement over privacy in tents on public land persists. United States v. Parkerson reinforced the restrictive Ruckman line but did not eliminate the contrary Sandoval and Gooch approach.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched tent public land reasonable expectation privacy split and Sandoval Ruckman camping; reviewed United States v. Parkerson and cited circuit authorities.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0077\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Circuits continue to disagree whether inevitable discovery requires active pursuit. The Steeprock petition presents the issue, but no certiorari grant was found as of 2026-07-21.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched inevitable discovery active pursuit split and Satterfield Nix circuits divide; reviewed Eagan, Villarreal, and the State v. Steeprock docket.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0078\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Smith's categorical general-warrant holding remains binding only in the Fifth Circuit. Chatrie held that geofence acquisition is a search but did not adopt Smith's categorical validity rule.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched geofence general warrant Fifth Circuit only and Smith categorical general warrant; reviewed United States v. Smith and Chatrie v. United States.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0079\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Chatrie v. United States expressly left probable cause and particularity for the Fourth Circuit on remand, so the Supreme Court still has not decided whether any geofence warrant can satisfy those requirements.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched geofence warrant probable cause particularity open; reviewed the Supreme Court's June 29, 2026 Chatrie opinion.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0080\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Ninth Circuit continues to require reasonable suspicion for forensic border-device searches, while the Eleventh Circuit permits suspicionless searches and other circuits use intermediate or reserved formulations.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched forensic border device search circuit split and Touset Cotterman reasonable suspicion; reviewed Cotterman, Cano, Pulido, Nkongho, and Mendez.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0081\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court still has not required individualized suspicion for border searches of property as distinct from highly intrusive searches of the person. Belmonte Cardozo expressly reiterated that point in 2026.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Supreme Court suspicion search property border and no SCOTUS suspicion border property; reviewed Flores-Montano and United States v. Belmonte Cardozo.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0082\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Recent circuit decisions continue to recognize differing suspicion requirements for electronic-device searches at the border; no Supreme Court resolution or relevant grant was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched border device search suspicion split and Touset Cotterman; reviewed Pulido, Nkongho, Mendez, Eta, and Belmonte Cardozo.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0083\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Tuggle was an issue of first impression in the Seventh Circuit, and the broader lower-court disagreement over prolonged pole-camera surveillance remains unresolved nationally.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched pole camera surveillance first impression split and Tuggle long-term pole camera; reviewed Tuggle, House, Hay, Harry, Gregory, and Green.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0084\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The characterization of Tuggle as the Seventh Circuit's first-impression decision remains accurate; United States v. House later reaffirmed rather than displaced Tuggle.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched prolonged pole camera first impression Seventh Circuit and Tuggle issue of first impression; reviewed Tuggle and United States v. House.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0085\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Federal circuits have largely rejected public-facing pole-camera claims, while contrary state high-court decisions and Moore-Bush's divided First Circuit disposition preserve the wider disagreement.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched pole camera Carpenter mosaic courts split and Tuggle unresolved tension; reviewed House, Hay, Harry, Gregory, Green, and Moore-Bush.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0086\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court has not clarified Payton's reason-to-believe quantum, and federal circuits continue to apply different certainty thresholds before officers may enter a dwelling to arrest a suspect.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Payton reason to believe quantum entry arrest and Vasquez-Algarin open question; reviewed Vasquez-Algarin, Brinkley, and later citations.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0087\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The acknowledged split persists between circuits equating Payton's reason-to-believe standard with probable cause and circuits treating it as a lesser standard.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Payton reason to believe probable cause split and Vasquez-Algarin acknowledged circuit split; checked recent appellate and Supreme Court authorities.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0088\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Wilson's narrow private-search approach now has support from Maher and Lowers, while Reddick, Miller, and Brillhart take the opposing hash-match view. The Supreme Court has not resolved the split.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched hash match private search split circuits and Wilson Reddick Miller; reviewed Maher, Lowers, and Brillhart.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0089\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later D.C. Circuit or Supreme Court decision was found deciding the suspicion threshold that Xiang avoided because reasonable suspicion existed on the facts.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Xiang forensic device search suspicion required and outbound border search reasonable suspicion; reviewed Xiang and later citing decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0090\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The precise standard for forensic device searches at the border remains divided among the circuits, with no Supreme Court merits resolution.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched forensic border device search standard split and Xiang unresolved circuit split; reviewed Cotterman, Cano, Kolsuz, Touset, Pulido, Mendez, and Nkongho.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0091\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No subsequent published disposition was found deciding whether Bradford's self-censorship constituted the completed injury that Uzuegbunam reserved.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Uzuegbunam Bradford completed injury and nominal damages self-censor standing; reviewed the Supreme Court opinion and later citations.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0092\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"On remand, the Tenth Circuit examined the corrected record concerning when White arrived and what he witnessed, held that a jury could find he witnessed or participated in the officers' threatening conduct, and ultimately granted qualified immunity.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Pauly v. White, 874 F.3d 1197 (10th Cir. 2017).\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched White v. Pauly alternative ground and Pauly remand what White witnessed; reviewed the Tenth Circuit's published remand opinion.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0093\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court later resolved the de novo question by holding that a child's age, when known or objectively apparent, is relevant to Miranda custody.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"J.D.B. v. North Carolina, 564 U.S. 261 (2011).\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched juvenile age Miranda custody de novo and Alvarado age J.D.B.; reviewed the Supreme Court opinion in J.D.B.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0094\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No recent Supreme Court decision was found materially revising the traditional due-process voluntariness framework; current development remains primarily in circuit and state courts.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched voluntariness confession no SCOTUS recent and due process voluntariness circuit developments; checked Supreme Court opinions and grants.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0095\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Lower courts continue to disagree about Seibert's fractured reasoning and apply Shatzer's break-in-custody rule to varied facts. No Supreme Court decision has unified those applications.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Seibert two-step deliberate circuits and Shatzer break in custody lower courts; reviewed recent circuit decisions and McCarthy v. Hernandez.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0096\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The split over whether Seibert's plurality or Justice Kennedy's concurrence controls remains live. No pending grant addressing that issue was found; McCarthy v. Hernandez did not resolve it.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Seibert Marks narrowest holding split and Capers Williams deliberate two-step; checked current Supreme Court dockets.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0097\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Circuits continue to differ over whether Quarles permits unwarned questioning after a suspect is handcuffed or otherwise secured, and no Supreme Court resolution was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched public safety exception secured arrestee split and Quarles scope secured suspect; reviewed current circuit authorities.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0098\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Liddell's express description of a circuit split remains accurate; later decisions have not produced a uniform rule governing the breadth of the public-safety exception.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched public safety exception circuit split Liddell and Quarles broad reading Eighth Circuit; checked later appellate cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0099\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court merits case or certiorari grant was found addressing the Garrity self-executing-immunity and public-employee warning questions identified by the claim.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Garrity self-executing immunity Supreme Court and Kalkines warning POBR; checked current Supreme Court dockets.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0100\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The circuits remain divided over whether Brady requires disclosure of material exculpatory evidence before a guilty plea. The Supreme Court has not resolved the issue or granted a current merits case.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Brady pre-plea disclosure circuits divided and Supreme Court pre-plea Brady; reviewed Ruiz, Alvarez, Mansfield, and current dockets.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0101\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The 2019 certiorari denial left the pre-plea Brady split intact, and no later Supreme Court decision or grant was found resolving it.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Brady pre-plea split cert denied and Alvarez split intact; checked later circuit cases and current Supreme Court dockets.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0102\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court still has not applied Russell's reserved outrageous-government-conduct theory to invalidate a conviction, although lower courts continue to recognize a very narrow potential defense.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched outrageous government conduct due process reserved and Russell Hampton defense; checked recent Supreme Court decisions and grants.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0103\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Recent federal appellate decisions continue to state the same two core entrapment elements: government inducement and lack of predisposition. Divergence concerns evidentiary application and subsidiary formulations, not the core rule.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched entrapment two-element test no split and Jacobson predisposition divergence; reviewed recent Hilliard, Crisler, and Warick decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0104\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No recent Supreme Court merits decision was found changing the federal eyewitness-identification framework; doctrinal development remains concentrated in circuit and state courts.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched eyewitness identification no SCOTUS recent and Manson reliability state high courts; checked Supreme Court opinions and grants.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0105\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court has not supplied a comprehensive rule identifying every post-attachment event that constitutes a critical stage requiring counsel after Rothgery.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Rothgery post-attachment critical stage counsel and which events require counsel; reviewed later Supreme Court Sixth Amendment decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0106\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The combined pole-camera, ALPR, and drone frontier remains scale- and jurisdiction-dependent. United States v. Porter resolved only limited ALPR use in the Fifth Circuit, while pole-camera and drone questions remain divided or undeveloped.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched pole camera ALPR drone frontier split and digital surveillance scale-dependent unsettled; reviewed Porter, House, Hay, Harry, Gregory, Green, and drone cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0107\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The border-device forensic-search split remains unresolved after recent Fourth, Seventh, and Eleventh Circuit decisions; no Supreme Court merits resolution or grant was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched border device forensic search split and Cotterman Cano Touset; reviewed Nkongho, Pulido, Mendez, Eta, and Belmonte Cardozo.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0108\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"On remand from Brownback, the Sixth Circuit held that the FTCA judgment bar applies to Bivens claims brought in the same lawsuit. The Supreme Court later denied certiorari.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"King v. United States, 49 F.4th 991 (6th Cir. 2022), cert. denied sub nom. King v. Brownback, 144 S. Ct. 10 (2023).\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched FTCA judgment bar Bivens same suit and Brownback King remand; reviewed the Sixth Circuit opinion and Supreme Court No. 22-912 disposition.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0109\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Carroll v. Carman remains the Supreme Court's last treatment and expressly declined the constitutional merits. No later Supreme Court decision or grant resolving which non-front entrances fall within the implied license was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Carroll v. Carman knock and talk front door and federal appellate Carroll side door; reviewed Carroll and later cases including United States v. McNeil.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0110\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Chatrie held that acquisition of geofence location data is a Fourth Amendment search but expressly left probable cause and particularity for the Fourth Circuit on remand.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Chatrie probable cause particularity 2026; reviewed the Supreme Court's official No. 25-112 opinion and docket.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0111\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"A state supreme court answered the reserved issue in a substantial field-fingerprinting setting, holding that fingerprinting people stopped without probable cause violated the Fourth Amendment because it exceeded Terry's permissible scope and duration. The decision did not categorically foreclose every tightly limited Hayes-style procedure.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Johnson v. VanderKooi, 509 Mich. 524, 983 N.W.2d 779 (2022).\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched field fingerprinting reasonable suspicion Hayes and Johnson v. VanderKooi fingerprinting Terry; reviewed the Michigan Supreme Court opinion.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0112\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court subsequently supplied the general rule for ordinary retaliatory-arrest claims in Nieves and clarified its objective-evidence exception in Gonzalez.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Nieves v. Bartlett, 587 U.S. 391 (2019); Gonzalez v. Trevino, 602 U.S. 653 (2024).\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Lozman Nieves retaliatory arrest elements and Gonzalez Trevino exception; reviewed both Supreme Court opinions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0113\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later Supreme Court merits decision defines universal elements for a fabricated-evidence claim or selects a single constitutional source. Recent circuit decisions remain source- and context-specific.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched McDonough fabricated evidence constitutional source and post-McDonough appellate decisions; reviewed Hoskins, Harris, Morphew, and Ciria.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0114\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"Later courts addressed the underlying retention issue. Asinor held continued retention must remain reasonable, and Richman directly held that later warrantless re-search of retained full computer images was unconstitutional and that ongoing retention lacked adequate safeguards.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Asinor v. District of Columbia, 111 F.4th 1249 (D.C. Cir. 2024); Richman v. United States, No. 1:25-mc-170, 2025 WL 3611753 (D.D.C. Dec. 12, 2025).\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Ganias retention nonresponsive data and Richman forensic image retention 2025; reviewed Asinor, Zelaya-Veliz, Cawthorn, and Richman.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0115\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later First Circuit decision resolves Moore-Bush's evenly divided en banc merits question, so the disposition still establishes no binding First Circuit rule.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched First Circuit Moore-Bush pole camera and Moore-Bush 2026; checked later First Circuit opinions and decisions from other circuits.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0116\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court still has not decided cellphone abandonment. Hunt, Small, Crumble, and Morgan apply fact-sensitive approaches that have not produced a recognized federal circuit split on device-versus-data abandonment.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched abandoned cell phone Fourth Amendment circuit and cellphone abandonment Supreme Court; reviewed Hunt, Small, Crumble, Morgan, and the Morgan certiorari docket.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0117\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later Supreme Court aerial-surveillance decision resolves Riley's reservations concerning physical disruption from an overflight or observation of intimate details.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Florida v. Riley intimate details reserved and Supreme Court aerial overflight physical effects; checked later Supreme Court surveillance cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0118\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Persistent fixed aerial surveillance and small-drone surveillance remain unsettled nationally. Leaders of a Beautiful Struggle resolves one aerial program, while pole-camera and state drone decisions remain technology- and fact-specific.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched persistent automated aerial surveillance Fourth Amendment and pole camera drone surveillance unsettled; reviewed Leaders of a Beautiful Struggle and recent pole-camera and drone cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0119\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No national controlling rule has emerged. Federal appellate courts increasingly reject public-facing pole-camera claims, while Moore-Bush and contrary state authority preserve disagreement; the Williamson petition was denied in 2026.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched pole camera lower courts divide 2025 and Williamson pole camera certiorari; reviewed Harry, Gregory, Green, House, Hay, Moore-Bush, and Supreme Court docket No. 25-412.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0120\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court still has not addressed Fourth Amendment drone surveillance, and no published federal circuit opinion was found deciding a drone-surveillance merits challenge. Existing federal drone cases concern other constitutional or evidentiary issues.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched federal appellate drone surveillance Fourth Amendment and Florida v. Riley drone surveillance; reviewed National Press, Muhammed, State v. Bradley, and Long Lake Township v. Maxon.\"}"}}
